{
  "title": "Radial and tangential dipole emission in a homogeneous medium (spherical multipoles, analytic)",
  "description": "Two current elements (moment 1e-12 A m) at r = 0.3 um in a homogeneous dielectric of n = 1.5, one radial, one tangential (em_fdfd_sphere dipole_source; each is the sum of its multipole terms: the radial one excites only the electric family with m = 0, the tangential one both families with m = +-1, and the two are solved apart since their m sets are orthogonal). Half disc to 0.6 um, 1 nm radial cells, 90 theta cells, the medium continued to infinity by the exact outgoing condition, multipole orders 1..16 (n k r ~ 2.8: the terms fall off fast beyond l ~ 6), frequency_domain study at 0.8, 1.0 and 1.2 um.\nAnalytic: an element in an unbounded medium emits P = eta k^2 (I d)^2 / (12 pi), eta = eta0 / n, k = n k0, i.e. n times its vacuum power, wherever it sits and whatever its orientation; the Purcell factor (power over that of the same medium) is 1.\nDerived values tab: P_radial / P_tangential at 1 um vs n eta0 k0^2 (I d)^2 / (12 pi) and their purcell series vs 1 (pins the angular couplings and the source normalisation; ~1e-4). Put a sphere of another index around the origin (a circle material) to see the Purcell factor change with the distance to its surface.",
  "version": 2,
  "parameters": [
    {
      "name": "n_m",
      "expression": 1.5,
      "description": "refractive index of the medium"
    },
    {
      "name": "r_d",
      "expression": "0.3*um",
      "description": "radius of both dipoles"
    },
    {
      "name": "r_out",
      "expression": "0.6*um",
      "description": "outer end of the radial line"
    },
    {
      "name": "dx_cell",
      "expression": "1*nm",
      "description": "uniform radial cell size"
    },
    {
      "name": "Id",
      "expression": 1e-12,
      "description": "current moment I d of both elements [A m]"
    },
    {
      "name": "lambda0",
      "expression": "1*um",
      "description": "reference wavelength of the derived values"
    },
    {
      "name": "k_vac",
      "expression": "2*pi/lambda0",
      "description": "vacuum wave number at lambda0 (k0 is the Bessel function K_0)"
    },
    {
      "name": "P_ref",
      "expression": "n_m*sqrt(mu0_const/eps0_const)*k_vac**2*Id**2/(12*pi)",
      "description": "power in the unbounded medium at lambda0"
    }
  ],
